Z||000000||85Josh||03-19-2001||10:04 PM||||I just got a 120mph speedo. I guess it must come from an 85, since it is reflective lit, like the exsisting gauges in my 85 2M4. Anyway, I am hoping someone can tell me how to change the odometer reading. My old one shows 149k, this one is 139k. I don't want to spend an entire month of spare time with a drill chucked to my VSS! Any help is appreciated.<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4<BR>CAVALRY SCOUT!(stuck in Field Artillery)||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||03-19-2001||10:32 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 85Josh:<BR><B>I just got a 120mph speedo. I guess it must come from an 85, since it is reflective lit, like the exsisting gauges in my 85 2M4. Anyway, I am hoping someone can tell me how to change the odometer reading. My old one shows 149k, this one is 139k. I don't want to spend an entire month of spare time with a drill chucked to my VSS! Any help is appreciated.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey Josh!<BR>It's easy to change the odo reading...<BR>I have even put one unit back to zero for fun!<BR>First, remove the four nuts holding the PC board on, then remove the board...<BR>From there it is all visual...<BR>Tear it apart and put it together a few times to get the feel for it...<BR>crash...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||skitime||03-19-2001||10:36 PM||||It is easy to dial one in.  I had one that was brown from smokers and I replaced it with the exact same mileage but clean white numbers.  Remove the spool and start turning.  You will figure it out.||||reg||1
Z||000003||85Josh||03-19-2001||10:39 PM||||Thanks, guys, I'll takle this tomorrow after work.<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4<BR>CAVALRY SCOUT!(stuck in Field Artillery)||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wolfhound||03-20-2001||05:42 AM||||It,s easy to just put your old odometer cylinder in the new speedometer.||||reg||1
Z||000005||stimpy||03-20-2001||09:56 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fierowrecker:<BR><B> Hey Josh!<BR>It's easy to change the odo reading...<BR>I have even put one unit back to zero for fun!<BR>crash...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Remind me not to buy a car from you guys...<BR>Isn't it, umm, I dunno, illegal to tamper with odometers?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Wolfhound||03-20-2001||10:06 AM||||I think 85Josh wants his to read correct. 149 rather than the 139 showing.||||reg||1
Z||000007||skitime||03-20-2001||10:09 AM||||It is illegal to change the mileage but not to replace with the same mileage. If you read closely nobody was changing the mileage of the car. In fact the question was asked because he was worried the new speedometer was less than the orginal and he wanted to make it the same. He could of just replaced it and lost 10,000 miles now that would be dishonest. Watch out for removed mattress tags too.<p>[This message has been edited by skitime (edited 03-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||862M4inCA||03-20-2001||10:42 AM||||hrm, I wonder what the law says if the car automatically reduces milage? Mine did...lost 100K miles. LOL The clock was reading somewhere in the range of 299K (a previous owner had to repair a broken temp needle and rolled the odometer up an unknown value) on a road trip and when it was about to hit 300K all the numbers except the 2 changed over. now I've got about 209K. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||skitime||03-20-2001||10:48 AM||||Darn, I would of been upset if my Fiero that earned 300,000 wasn't displaying its well earned badge of durability.  You often win a prize at a Fiero show for most miles.||||reg||1
Z||000010||862M4inCA||03-20-2001||10:53 AM||||well it hasn't even made it to 300K originally. Judging from the amount of wear inside the vehicle, and age I would guess somewhere in the neighborhood of 140-180 original miles. I've had cars with 300K miles on them and this one, if it really does have that much, looks exceptional for that many miles. Not to mention the motor looked REAL good when I pulled it for a rebuild. Had a little cylinder wall wear tho the crosshatch was still noticable, and the bearing surfaces weren't worn all that bad. Must have had incredible maintainence if that engine had 300K on it.||||reg||1
Z||000011||skitime||03-20-2001||11:04 AM||||Makes you wonder who has the most actual miles on a Fiero. I guess there is a thread on that somewhere.  I test drove a really sharp 86 GT with 214,000.  Someone had really taken car of it. Had redone seats and headliner but everything else seemed orginal. Even the paint looked orginal and no paint fade.  I was impressed. They only wanted $600 for it.  It went to auction today.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Monkeyman||03-20-2001||12:31 PM||||85Josh--There was no 120mph speedo in any of the '85s.  Also, the 120mph speedos weren't reflector lit, they were backlit.||||reg||1
Z||000013||WKDFIRO||03-20-2001||12:45 PM||||I have a 120 mph speedo in my 85 GT and I have not been able to tell any difference between back lit and reflector lit. It seems identical with the tach and the tach is still stock.<P>As for mileage, I have always wondered the validity for any Fiero after I found out how mindbogglingly easy it is to tamper with it!||||reg||1
